Hello,

I'm the business manager at a secondary school of about 1,100 pupils, and our=
 governors have
asked me to review how visitors sign in. A primary school nearby uses your ki=
osk, and
their office spoke well of it. Before I contact anyone about prices, I'd like=
 to explain what we
need.

Our reception. One office at the main entrance, staffed from 7:45 to 16:30 by=
 two
receptionists. Visitors sign a paper book and get a sticker with their name w=
ritten on it.
Contractors are meant to read our safeguarding leaflet first, but in practice=
 many don't.

Who comes in. On a normal day: about 15 contractors and suppliers, a handful =
of visiting
professionals (social workers, nurses, educational psychologists), and parent=
s for meetings. On
parents' evenings and open days it's several hundred people in two hours, whi=
ch the paper book
can't cope with at all.

Safeguarding. This is our priority. Every visitor has to be identified and ba=
dged, and anyone
who will be with pupils needs their checks confirmed by our designated safegu=
arding lead before
they go beyond reception. We'd want the kiosk to flag those visitors for her =
rather than let
them straight in.

Fire safety. In a fire drill, the receptionists take the paper book to the as=
sembly point. It
is slow and often out of date. A printed or on-screen list of who is in the b=
uilding right now
would be a real improvement.

Pupils. Pupils who arrive late sign in at a separate window today. I'm not as=
king about that;
it's handled by our attendance software and we'd keep it as it is.

Our IT. The school's IT is run by the trust. They'd need to approve anything =
connected to the
network, and they'll ask where visitor records are stored and for how long. W=
e're also required
to delete records we no longer need.

Budget. We'd fund this from next year's budget, so nothing would happen befor=
e April. I want the
governors to see a clear option at their spring meeting.

Accessibility. A number of our visitors, and some parents, don't read English=
 well, and a few
use wheelchairs. Whatever we choose would need to be usable by them without a=
 receptionist
standing over them, though staff will always be close by. The governors will =
also ask about the
cost of looking after it: who fixes it when it breaks, and how quickly. I'll =
come back to those
questions later, once I know the basic setup would work for us.

Parents. Our receptionists' biggest daily task, apart from contractors, is pa=
rents collecting
children early for appointments. At the moment they sign a separate sheet, an=
d the office checks
the child is allowed to leave with them.

My question for now: can parents collecting a child early sign in and out at =
the same kiosk as
other visitors, or does it need a separate setup?

Kind regards,
Martin Achebe
Business manager, Oakfield Academy
